  INSERT INTO lpm_threads (id, project_id, branch_id, slug, title, thread_type, explained_by_element_id, connecting_idea, narrative, teaching_prompt, evidence_note, gaps, status)
  VALUES ('e36d6905-38cf-51b0-be37-0959e222d155', v_proj, v_branch, 'cosmic-story-of-life', $J$The Story of Life$J$, 'vertical', '77cc800e-5b91-588c-b9e8-343e50435021', $J$How has life on Earth changed, and what does 'change' mean across such enormous stretches of time?$J$, $J$Montessori's Second Great Lesson: the appearance and change of life on Earth across deep time, told through the Timeline of Life and the Clock of Eras — plants and animals appearing, changing, and often disappearing across the Paleozoic, Mesozoic, and Cenozoic. Where Cosmic Education's own affective "Cosmic Task" framing (every living thing plays a role serving the whole) meets evolutionary biology's own hard-won distinction between legitimate and illegitimate purposive language most directly — this strand names that tension explicitly at GB-3 rather than leaving it implicit, the strand-specific reason this LPM's README treats it as a genuine open research question.$J$, $J$Montessori's Cosmic Task says every living thing has a role that serves the whole. A biologist says a trait persists because it helped past generations survive and reproduce, not because anything needed it. Are these the same idea told two ways, or genuinely different claims — and does it matter which one a child comes away believing?$J$, $J$Built from the cosmic-education-k12 draft's strand text and essential questions. No expert has reviewed it. Montessori's own scope is ages 6 to 12; the draft calls the progression a designed thought experiment that has not been tested against children's reasoning.$J$, '[]'::jsonb, 'proposed')
  ON CONFLICT (project_id, slug) DO NOTHING;
