  INSERT INTO lpm_threads (id, project_id, branch_id, slug, title, thread_type, explained_by_element_id, connecting_idea, narrative, teaching_prompt, evidence_note, gaps, status)
  VALUES ('7775668d-9294-50f7-85ad-62edbf1b318b', v_proj, v_branch, 'cosmic-story-of-humans', $J$The Story of Humans$J$, 'vertical', 'cab10790-5d51-5768-a567-df0ec9aca67d', $J$What do all humans need, and how differently have people met those same needs?$J$, $J$Montessori's Third Great Lesson: the coming of humans, the timeline of hominids, and — its own named organizing device — Fundamental Human Needs (physical: food, shelter, defense, transportation; spiritual/psychological: art, religion, music, a sense of significance) met in different ways by every human culture across every period of history. The strand where Cosmic Education's own cross-cultural, multiply-realized-solutions framing runs structurally parallel to evolutionary anthropology's own treatment of universal human needs and variable cultural solutions — the specific parallel this LPM's own synthesis draws to eva_buch (Hanisch, forthcoming), not a claim either source makes about the other.$J$, $J$Every known human group has had to solve the same handful of problems — food, shelter, safety, meaning. A class comparing how three very different cultures solved 'shelter' is doing something evolutionary anthropology also does with 'cooperation' or 'fairness' — what changes, and what stays the same, when the same comparative method is applied to a physical need versus a social one?$J$, $J$Built from the cosmic-education-k12 draft's strand text and essential questions. No expert has reviewed it. Montessori's own scope is ages 6 to 12; the draft calls the progression a designed thought experiment that has not been tested against children's reasoning.$J$, '[]'::jsonb, 'proposed')
  ON CONFLICT (project_id, slug) DO NOTHING;
