  INSERT INTO lpm_threads (id, project_id, branch_id, slug, title, thread_type, explained_by_element_id, connecting_idea, narrative, teaching_prompt, evidence_note, gaps, status)
  VALUES ('015b5582-4827-5868-be22-c072970598a5', v_proj, v_branch, 'cosmic-story-of-language', $J$The Story of Language$J$, 'vertical', '4169768d-db96-5f05-a337-668ed11d0a0f', $J$How did marks on a surface come to carry meaning, and what changed once they could?$J$, $J$Montessori's Fourth Great Lesson: how humans came to represent sounds and ideas with arbitrary marks — signs, pictographs, alphabets — and how that single invention let knowledge accumulate and transmit across generations beyond what direct memory could carry. Read alongside oe-interdisciplinary-k12's Strand 204, which treats analogical reasoning and the "growth of understanding" as a shared pattern across individual minds, cultures, and the history of science itself — this strand supplies that pattern's own historical origin story for the specific case of written language.$J$, $J$Every writing system humans have ever invented uses arbitrary marks agreed on by a group — nothing about the shape of a letter 'A' makes it mean what it means. What does a class gain by comparing several real writing systems side by side, instead of treating the one they're learning as the natural or obvious one?$J$, $J$Built from the cosmic-education-k12 draft's strand text and essential questions. No expert has reviewed it. Montessori's own scope is ages 6 to 12; the draft calls the progression a designed thought experiment that has not been tested against children's reasoning.$J$, '[]'::jsonb, 'proposed')
  ON CONFLICT (project_id, slug) DO NOTHING;
